<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<!--
  dev.ailang.mission-control.plist — nightly V1-mission outer-loop iteration.

  Fires a headless Fable session at 22:00 that runs ONE mission-control
  iteration (design_docs/v1-mission.md): pick top backlog item, route through
  design-doc → sprint-plan → execute (Opus) → evaluate, record, retro.
  6h hard timeout in the driver → done by ~04:00, before the 04:00-07:00 eval
  blackout window ever matters (this job is cloud-only and takes NO rig lock —
  rig.lock is a GPU mutex; see the two-tier rule in the charter).

  Installation (one-time, deliberate — this arms an autonomous nightly loop):
    cp tools/launchd/dev.ailang.mission-control.plist ~/Library/LaunchAgents/
    launchctl load ~/Library/LaunchAgents/dev.ailang.mission-control.plist

  Test dry-run (no tokens spent, checks wiring):
    MISSION_DRY_RUN=1 bash tools/launchd/mission-control.sh

  Kick one iteration manually:
    launchctl kickstart gui/$UID/dev.ailang.mission-control

  Kill switch (survives reboots, no unload needed):
    touch ~/.ailang/state/mission-control.disabled

  Logs: /tmp/ailang-mission-control.log

  Dependencies:
    - claude CLI at ~/.local/bin/claude (authenticated)
    - gh authenticated as sunholo-voight-kampff (plaintext storage for headless:
      gh auth login \-\-insecure-storage — keychain tokens are unreadable here)
    - ailang binary at ~/go/bin/ailang (messages CLI for the morning report)
-->
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
    <key>Label</key>
    <string>dev.ailang.mission-control</string>

    <key>ProgramArguments</key>
    <array>
        <string>/bin/bash</string>
        <string>/Users/voightkampff/dev/sunholo-data/ailang/tools/launchd/mission-control.sh</string>
    </array>

    <!-- Continuous mode (2026-07-10, Mark): the driver's overlap guard yields while
         an iteration is still running, so this is "back-to-back iterations, at most
         one interval of idle gap". No GPU/rig-lock clash — iterations are cloud-model
         work; subscription rate limits are the natural throttle. Revert to a
         StartCalendarInterval block for nightly-only cadence.
         REVERTED to 90m (Mark, attended 2026-09-02) after ONE day at 60m. The 60m trial
         and its rationale are kept below because the measurement that killed it is the
         useful part:
           date         starts  stalls  rate
           2026-08-31       18       1     6%
           2026-09-02       18       6    33%   <- first full day at the tightened cadence
         Every stall was the same signature — "claude idle with a descendant alive >=2400s"
         — so each burned 40min before the watchdog killed it, and 3 of v1's 7 fires died
         that way. Total starts did NOT rise (19 -> 18): we paid the contention and got no
         extra throughput. Confound checked and rejected: the attended git/merge work that
         day ran 09:35-11:20 and ALL SIX stalls fell between 14:02 and 18:42.
         Mechanism (inferred, not proven): tightening v1+world+docs together made three
         controllers overlap against one quota bucket, sub-agents slowed, controllers sat
         idle, the watchdog fired. The original 60m rationale still stands on its own terms
         — there IS a flat ~90min idle gap per cycle — but the gap is not the binding
         constraint; the bucket and the box are. Do not re-tighten one loop without holding
         the other two, and re-read the stall rate the next day. -->
    <!-- KeepAlive + ThrottleInterval, NOT StartInterval (2026-09-05, Mark attended:
         "fix this, especially for ailang v1 and world").
         MEASURED FIRST, then changed. StartInterval re-arms from the job's EXIT, not its
         start — three consecutive v1 gaps in ~/.ailang/state/mission-v1-slot-verdicts.log
         measured 5403s, 5372s and 5379s against a 5400s interval, and world independently
         gave 14390s against 14400s. So the real period was duration + interval, and v1's
         duration (mean 5479s over 5 slots) is almost exactly its interval: HALF of v1's
         day was idle, 11.9h, none of it chosen. This plist's own text has said since
         2026-07-10 that the intent was "back-to-back iterations, at most one interval of
         idle gap" — the scheduler was never delivering that.
         ThrottleInterval is measured from the START, so the period becomes
         max(duration, 5400) instead of duration + 5400: the NOMINAL SPACING IS UNCHANGED
         at the 90m Mark ratified on 09-02, only the wasted slot goes. Projected 7.9 ->
         15.8 iterations/day.
         ThrottleInterval is load-bearing, not decoration: the yield paths (kill switch,
         overlap guard, memory gate, dry run) exit in seconds, and bare KeepAlive would
         respawn them in a tight loop. It floors that at one start per 90m.
         NOT a licence to re-tighten. The 09-02 measurement below stands — three loops
         tightened at once took the fleet stall rate 6% -> 33% and returned no extra
         starts. This changes ONE loop and does not touch the interval. Re-read the stall
         rate the next day; the signature is "claude idle with a descendant alive >=2400s".
         Revert = delete these two keys and restore <key>StartInterval</key> 5400. -->
    <key>KeepAlive</key>
    <true/>
    <key>ThrottleInterval</key>
    <integer>5400</integer>

    <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
    <dict>
        <key>PATH</key>
        <string>/Users/voightkampff/go/bin:/Users/voightkampff/.local/bin:/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
        <key>HOME</key>
        <string>/Users/voightkampff</string>
    </dict>

    <key>WorkingDirectory</key>
    <string>/Users/voightkampff/dev/sunholo-data/ailang</string>

    <key>StandardOutPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/ailang-mission-control.stdout</string>

    <key>StandardErrorPath</key>
    <string>/tmp/ailang-mission-control.stderr</string>

    <!-- RunAtLoad=true (2026-07-21): fire once at every login/boot so a reboot can never silently kill the cadence (18h outage 07-20); the kill switch still gates deliberate offs. Redundant under KeepAlive (which starts it
         at load anyway) but kept so the two can be reverted independently. -->
    <key>RunAtLoad</key>
    <true/>
</dict>
</plist>
